  { q: "Was Wu Zetian really the only female emperor in Chinese history?", a: "Mainstream historical scholarship and standard reference works describe her as the only undisputed female sovereign in Chinese history, a position Wikipedia and Britannica both reflect. The word \"undisputed\" is doing real work: historians debate a few earlier contested cases, so this is a scholarly consensus rather than a claim with no edges." },
  { q: "What is the difference between \"Empress Wu\" and \"Emperor Wu Zetian\"?", a: "\"Empress Wu\" (or Wuhou, 武后) refers to her period as an emperor's consort and later dowager, when she exercised power through her husband and sons. In the standard dating she then reigned as emperor in her own right from 690 to 705, with her own dynastic name and era names — a different and more unusual thing." },
  { q: "When did Wu Zetian live and rule?", a: "She is usually dated 624–705 CE: Britannica gives her birth in 624 at Wenshui in Shanxi and her death on 16 December 705 at Luoyang, and public-history accounts commonly give her reign as emperor as 690–705. Exact figures vary slightly between sources, so treat them as the standard dating." },
  { q: "Did Wu Zetian invent new Chinese characters?", a: "It is commonly held that a set of new character forms, often called 则天文字 (Zétiān wénzì), was promulgated in connection with her reign, including 曌 in her own personal name. The Palace Museum publishes scholarly discussion of these \"new characters\", and specialists still debate how many there were and exactly which forms count." },
  { q: "Which Western ruler is Wu Zetian most like?", a: "Elizabeth I of England and Catherine the Great of Russia are useful orientation points, since all three held sovereign power in their own name and were attacked in gendered terms for it. Structurally, Empress Irene of Byzantium — who ruled as sole sovereign — is a closer parallel, because Wu Zetian did not simply inherit an existing throne." },
  { q: "How much of what I've read about Wu Zetian is actually history?", a: "Her offices, dates and reign are documented in historical records and discussed in modern scholarship, but many lurid personal stories come from much later fiction, opera and television drama, or from traditional writers who treated a woman's rule as illegitimate by definition. Separate the documented career from the storytelling built on top of it." }
];
